# Janus kernel config for Raspberry Pi 4/5 (BCM2711/BCM2712), aarch64.
# Single Board Computer tranche (docs/companion-site-builder-scope.md).
# Proven: rootfs/init (GOARCH=arm64) boots as PID 1 and prints
# JANUS_INIT_BOOT_OK under QEMU's raspi4b machine (see
# hack/qemu-raspi4-boot-test.sh) - the direct aarch64 analog of the
# original x86 Phase 1 boot-proof milestone. Real hardware (SD/MMC boot
# media, USB, BCM GENET ethernet, the pftf/RPi4 UEFI firmware) is NOT
# yet covered - this is deliberately scoped to the smallest provable
# console-boot increment first, mirroring how x86 Phase 1 began.
#
# Built from `make ARCH=arm64 allnoconfig` (NOT upstream's own broad
# `defconfig`, which enables every ARM SoC vendor's drivers at once and
# turned out to actively crash under QEMU's raspi4b machine - see
# below) plus exactly the symbols needed for that boot. Regenerate via
# `make ARCH=arm64 CROSS_COMPILE=aarch64-linux-gnu- olddefconfig` after
# hand-editing, same convention as ../janus_defconfig (x86_64) -
# deliberately the FULL resolved config (every symbol explicit), not a
# compact savedefconfig diff, for the same reason documented there: a
# diff format resolves unlisted symbols against the kernel's own
# defconfig-derived defaults, which would silently re-enable drivers
# allnoconfig turned off.
#
# fragment.config (the union merged onto allnoconfig to get here):
#
#   # BCM2711 SoC platform - CONFIG_ARCH_BCM2835 is gated behind a
#   # `menuconfig ARCH_BCM` bool, the exact same "gating menu" Kconfig
#   # trap as x86's own CONFIG_VIRTIO_BLK needing CONFIG_BLK_DEV=y first
#   # (see ../janus_defconfig's own header) - both are needed explicitly.
#   CONFIG_ARCH_BCM=y CONFIG_ARCH_BCM2835=y
#
#   # boot-proof baseline (console, initramfs, devtmpfs, binfmt_elf) -
#   # CONFIG_BINFMT_ELF is off by default under allnoconfig; without it
#   # *any* ELF binary (including this project's own Go rootfs/init)
#   # fails to exec as PID 1 with ENOEXEC ("Failed to execute /init
#   # (error -8)") - found by a real boot, not assumed from x86's own
#   # fragment already listing it (allnoconfig's defaults differ per arch).
#   CONFIG_ARM_AMBA=y CONFIG_SERIAL_AMBA_PL011=y
#   CONFIG_SERIAL_AMBA_PL011_CONSOLE=y CONFIG_SMP=y
#   CONFIG_BLK_DEV_INITRD=y CONFIG_DEVTMPFS=y CONFIG_DEVTMPFS_MOUNT=y
#   CONFIG_TMPFS=y CONFIG_PROC_FS=y CONFIG_SYSFS=y
#   CONFIG_COMPAT_32BIT_TIME=y CONFIG_BINFMT_ELF=y
#
# Two real, previously-undiscovered QEMU raspi4b emulation gaps found
# empirically (a real boot crashing with "Internal error: synchronous
# external abort" - a genuine bus fault, not a Kconfig gap), neither
# fixable by a driver Kconfig change alone without losing real-hardware
# functionality entirely:
#
#   # The "AON" L2 interrupt controller at 0x7ef00100 (brcm,l2-intc,
#   # compatible "brcm,bcm2711-l2-intc") is used only for HDMI/DVP/VEC
#   # display-adjacent interrupts - QEMU's raspi4b doesn't model this
#   # MMIO region at all, and probing it panics the boot
#   # (brcmstb_l2_intc_probe). This headless reverse-proxy appliance has
#   # no display driver at all, so it's disabled outright, not just for
#   # QEMU - real hardware doesn't need it for this project's use case.
#   CONFIG_BRCMSTB_L2_IRQ is not set (default y for ARCH_BCM2835,
#   explicitly disabled: `scripts/config --disable BRCMSTB_L2_IRQ`)
#
#   # The generic `clk_disable_unused` initcall (runs once at boot,
#   # walking the FULL registered clock tree to gate off anything
#   # unused) also hit a real external abort probing some BCM2835
#   # clock-gate register QEMU doesn't emulate (clk_gate_is_enabled).
#   # NOT a Kconfig fix - this isn't a specific driver, it's a generic
#   # kernel initcall - fixed via the well-known `clk_ignore_unused`
#   # kernel command-line parameter instead (see
#   # hack/qemu-raspi4-boot-test.sh), which skips the walk entirely.
#
# (i2c-brcmstb also externally-aborted the same way under upstream's
# broad defconfig - moot here since starting from allnoconfig never
# enables CONFIG_I2C's subsystem menu in the first place.)
#
# Phase-2-equivalent follow-up: janusd needs a real TCP listen socket
# for its own gRPC server (and haproxy needs AF_UNIX for its stats
# socket) even with no NIC/route anywhere on this machine type -
# CONFIG_NET was entirely unset (Phase 1's boot proof never touched a
# socket at all), so the very first bind() failed outright with
# "socket: function not implemented" (ENOSYS - the socket(2) syscall
# itself, not a permission or routing problem) on a real boot, not
# assumed ahead of time. Added CONFIG_NET/INET/UNIX/PACKET=y (`scripts/
# config --enable NET --enable INET --enable UNIX --enable PACKET` then
# `make ARCH=arm64 olddefconfig`, the same fragment-then-resolve
# discipline as everything else in this file) - verified via the same
# full-resolved-config-diff discipline ../janus_defconfig's own KSPP/
# SELinux blocks already established: CONFIG_NET=y pulls in the entire
# Networking-options menu becoming *visible*, but almost everything in
# it still resolves to "not set" (no netfilter, no bridging, no
# NETDEVICES - correctly so, there is no NIC driver here at all and
# none is needed for a loopback-only gRPC listener); the only real
# auto-selected dependents are CONFIG_IPV6=y (kernel's own INET
# default), CONFIG_TCP_CONG_CUBIC=y, and CONFIG_BPF=y (NET's own hard
# kernel-internal dependency, unrelated to bpf(2) being usable from
# userspace). See hack/qemu-raspi4-daemon-test.sh for the real boot
# proof (janusd's own "listening on ... (mTLS required)" line and
# haproxy's own startup banner both appearing on the console).
#
# Phase-3-equivalent follow-up: QEMU's raspi4b machine emulates neither
# PCIe nor BCM GENET Ethernet at all, so no test on that machine type
# could ever give real HTTP proof the way x86's own qemu-network-test
# does - every rpi4 test so far has had to document that as a known,
# accepted limitation. Rather than waiting on real Pi hardware (or BCM
# GENET driver work) to close that gap, this same defconfig ALSO boots
# under QEMU's generic aarch64 "virt" machine - a synthetic board with
# no Pi-specific hardware at all, but real virtio-net/virtio-blk over a
# real PCIe root complex, giving genuine end-to-end network proof back.
# Not a replacement for real Pi hardware support (no BCM2711-specific
# driver is exercised this way at all) - a deliberately separate,
# narrower goal: prove the *janusd/haproxy/dm-verity/rootfs* pipeline
# itself works on aarch64 for real, decoupled from Pi-specific hardware
# enablement, which stays its own, later effort.
#
# Added CONFIG_PCI/PCI_HOST_GENERIC (virt's own PCIe host bridge),
# CONFIG_VIRTIO_PCI+VIRTIO_MMIO (either transport `-M virt` might use,
# confirmed empirically which one QEMU actually picks rather than
# guessing), CONFIG_VIRTIO_NET, and CONFIG_VIRTIO_BLK (for a later
# dm-verity root, added now since it's the same category of change and
# worth resolving in one pass, matching this file's own established
# "one clean full-diff per change" discipline). Two gating-menu traps,
# the *same* class already documented above for CONFIG_ARCH_BCM and in
# ../janus_defconfig for CONFIG_VIRTIO_BLK - a first attempt enabling
# just CONFIG_VIRTIO_NET/CONFIG_VIRTIO_BLK directly had `olddefconfig`
# silently reset both back to "not set", because their own parent
# menus (CONFIG_NETDEVICES, CONFIG_BLK_DEV) were still off and
# `olddefconfig` never auto-enables an invisible menu's own gate: fixed
# by enabling CONFIG_NETDEVICES/CONFIG_BLK_DEV explicitly too, found by
# checking the actual resolved output, not assumed. Verified via the
# same full-resolved-config-diff discipline as every other addition
# here: the newly-visible NET_VENDOR_*/WLAN_VENDOR_* symbols are all
# just organizational submenu gates (harmless, Kconfig's own default
# for an entered-but-otherwise-empty vendor menu) - no actual per-chip
# NIC/WLAN driver got enabled, only VIRTIO_NET/VIRTIO_BLK themselves.
# CONFIG_IP_PNP/CONFIG_IP_PNP_DHCP were *also* needed (a two-line diff,
# found right after the above: the virtio-net PCI device enumerated
# fine - `pci 0000:00:01.0: [1af4:1000]` - but a real boot logged
# "Unknown kernel command line parameters ip=dhcp, will be passed to
# user space", since the kernel didn't recognize `ip=dhcp` as a
# parameter at all without IP_PNP built in) - see hack/
# qemu-arm64-network-test.sh for the real HTTP-200 proof this
# unblocked.
#
# NOTE (2026-09-29): a documentation mistake, caught and fixed here -
# the IP_PNP addition immediately above was originally applied by
# copying `make olddefconfig`'s raw output straight over this file
# (`cp janus_rpi4_defconfig.new janus_rpi4_defconfig`), which silently
# discarded this entire custom header block down to this note, even
# though the resolved *config itself* was never affected (confirmed
# against git history: every CONFIG_* line survived correctly, only
# the leading comment block was lost) - the real commit that landed
# this hole is `6ba0233`. Every earlier change in this file properly
# merged a header addition with `make olddefconfig`'s own output (see
# each paragraph's own "regenerate via" convention); this one skipped
# that step. Reconstructed from this conversation's own prior turns
# (the exact text had already been written once, just never
# correctly saved) rather than rewritten from memory, and the
# "always merge header + resolved output, never blind-copy .new over
# the original" rule is now the one being followed for every change
# below this note too.
#
# Bare-metal-equivalent follow-up (aarch64 UEFI/UKI, Single Board
# Computer tranche cont'd): the arm64 kernel `Image` binary is already
# a directly-runnable UEFI PE/COFF executable on its own once
# CONFIG_EFI_STUB=y is set (unlike x86, arm64 has no separate
# "handover protocol" concept at all - CONFIG_EFI_HANDOVER_PROTOCOL
# doesn't even exist as a Kconfig symbol for this arch, confirmed
# empirically rather than assumed from x86's own defconfig). Added
# CONFIG_ACPI/EFI/EFI_STUB (UEFI boot itself), CONFIG_MD/BLK_DEV_DM/
# DM_INIT/DM_VERITY (dm-verity root), CONFIG_EXT4_FS (the persistent
# STATE partition), CONFIG_MISC_FILESYSTEMS+SQUASHFS+SQUASHFS_XZ (the
# read-only rootfs) and CONFIG_CRYPTO_SHA256 (dm-verity's own hash
# algorithm) - matching ../janus_defconfig's own equivalent additions
# symbol-for-symbol where they overlap. SQUASHFS hit the *exact* same
# gating-menu trap already documented in ../janus_defconfig's own
# header (CONFIG_MISC_FILESYSTEMS must be enabled first, or
# `olddefconfig` drops the whole menu silently) - found by checking the
# resolved output missing SQUASHFS entirely on the first attempt, not
# assumed from the x86 precedent alone. Verified via the same
# full-resolved-config-diff discipline as every prior addition: ACPI
# pulled in CPU_IDLE/THERMAL/DMI/PNP-ACPI as real, expected dependents
# (the same shape x86's own ACPI enablement already documented), EFI
# pulled in EFI_GENERIC_STUB/EFI_ARMSTUB_DTB_LOADER/EFI_EARLYCON (all
# arm64-specific EFI-stub plumbing, expected), and nothing unrelated
# came along either time.
#
# Real Raspberry Pi 4 hardware follow-up (2026-09-29, user-requested:
# "continue jusqu'à obtenir une image [...] je testerai sur une Pi4 et
# Pi5" - build a real, flashable image for the user to boot-test on
# real hardware, not just QEMU). Adds the two peripherals this
# appliance actually needs to function as a real reverse proxy once
# booted from real Pi4 firmware (image/rpi-uefi/, its own bullet in
# CLAUDE.md) - SD card storage and Ethernet - deliberately skipping
# USB/GPIO/thermal/display entirely, since this headless, no-shell
# appliance has no genuine use for any of them.
#
# CONFIG_MMC/MMC_SDHCI/MMC_SDHCI_PLTFM/MMC_SDHCI_IPROC=y: the SD card
# controller. Confirmed directly against this project's own pinned
# kernel source (drivers/mmc/host/sdhci-iproc.c, not assumed from
# secondary documentation) that this exact driver carries a real
# `CONFIG_ACPI`-gated `acpi_device_id` table matching `BCM2847`
# (BCM2711's Arasan-derived SD host controller) and `BRCME88C` (its
# eMMC2 controller) - the same driver Device Tree already uses via
# `brcm,bcm2711-emmc2`/Arasan `compatible` strings, just reached through
# its parallel ACPI id table instead, which is what pftf's own ACPI
# tables expose (`image/rpi-uefi/`'s own bullet). Both of this driver's
# gating deps (`ARCH_BCM2835 || ARCH_BRCMSTB || ARCH_BCM_IPROC`, `OF ||
# ACPI`) were already satisfied by this file's own earlier additions
# (`CONFIG_ARCH_BCM2835`/`CONFIG_ACPI` above) - no new gating-menu trap
# this time, `CONFIG_MMC_BLOCK` (the actual `/dev/mmcblk0` block device
# node) came along automatically as `MMC`'s own `default y` once the
# `menuconfig MMC` gate itself was entered.
#
# CONFIG_BCMGENET=y: the on-SoC Gigabit Ethernet MAC - HAProxy's whole
# reason for existing needs a real NIC. Same discipline: confirmed
# directly against drivers/net/ethernet/broadcom/genet/bcmgenet.c that
# it carries a real ACPI id (`BCM6E4E`, mapped to `bcm2711_plat_data`)
# alongside its Device-Tree-only `of_device_id` table - again reached
# via pftf's own ACPI tables, not Device Tree. Verified via the same
# full-resolved-config-diff discipline as every earlier addition here:
# pulls in exactly its own real dependency chain (`PHYLIB`, `FIXED_PHY`,
# `BROADCOM_PHY`/`BCM7XXX_PHY` - Pi4's actual on-board PHY vendor,
# `MDIO_BCM_UNIMAC`, `FWNODE_MDIO`/`OF_MDIO`/`ACPI_MDIO`) plus a long
# list of every *other* vendor's PHY driver correctly staying "not set"
# - nothing unrelated came along. `CONFIG_NET_SELFTESTS=y` and
# `# CONFIG_RPMB is not set` are the only two other newly-visible
# symbols, both inert (a test-only default-y once `NET`/`MMC` are on,
# and an unused eMMC security feature respectively).
#
# Deliberately NOT attempted here, and flagged as a real, upstream-
# kernel-level limitation rather than a gap this project can Kconfig
# its way around: Raspberry Pi 5 (BCM2712)'s own SD/Ethernet peripherals
# live behind its new "RP1" I/O companion chip, and this project's
# pinned 6.18.53 kernel's own MAINTAINERS file confirms RP1 support is
# currently limited to clock (`drivers/clk/clk-rp1.c`), misc/MFD
# (`drivers/misc/rp1/`), and pinctrl (`drivers/pinctrl/pinctrl-rp1.c`)
# - no RP1 MMC or Ethernet driver exists in mainline Linux at all yet,
# confirmed by grepping the real MAINTAINERS file directly, not assumed
# from secondary sources. This means a Pi5 image built from this same
# from-scratch, mainline-only kernel can prove firmware/UEFI/kernel
# boot (`image/rpi-uefi/`'s own Pi5 variant) but cannot reach a working
# SD-card-root/networked appliance state at all today, regardless of
# firmware or Kconfig choice - see that file's own bullet for the full
# reasoning and what was verified instead. Raspberry Pi's own downstream
# kernel fork does carry RP1 MMC/Ethernet support, but adopting it would
# mean abandoning this project's pinned-mainline-kernel build story for
# at least this one board - a real, standing decision point, not
# silently decided here.
# Node network configuration (2026-09-30): nodes can have several
# interfaces and 802.1Q VLANs, configured by janusd over rtnetlink.
# `scripts/config --enable VLAN_8021Q` then olddefconfig; the full
# resolved diff is exactly VLAN_8021Q=y plus its GVRP/MVRP options
# staying off (no dynamic VLAN registration protocols).
#   CONFIG_VLAN_8021Q=y
#
# Optional modules (2026-10-01): the same nftables and policy routing /
# ECMP symbols as janus_defconfig (see its header for the list), then
# olddefconfig; the full resolved diff is them plus the same
# dependencies - and SYN_COOKIES=y, which NETFILTER_SYNPROXY selects and
# this config lacked (init's tcp_syncookies hardening sysctl now applies
# here too).
#
# keepalived extension (2026-10-01): inotify for its track_file - the
# full resolved diff is INOTIFY_USER=y and FSNOTIFY=y, which it selects.
#   CONFIG_INOTIFY_USER=y
#
